<p>On Monday, Apple announced <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apple-announces-record-114-5b-fourth-quarter-results/">its fiscal fourth quarter results</a>, setting an all-time quarterly revenue record and closing its record fiscal year off with a bang. I made some charts to illustrate the thing, as I tend to do.</p>
<p>Now that Apple&#8217;s fiscal 2026 has concluded, it&#8217;s time for a second set of colorful financial charts, ones that take the longer view and show the changes in Apple&#8217;s business over an entire year.</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s dig into the charts, starting with the big one, overall Apple revenue over the last 15 years:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/8f16d3dc744f96c3a1554f15ef18808d.png?s=lY9LTsQwDIbv4i1NU6RBSJUQR2DBEs3CBHdaJrVD7AKjUe9OShCPJZssvvh_nQFVyRR6GM2S9t7r9B4kStY2yOzfkgvCRmzeRppJf_59lW7AVeLCiLkQaOAJDaE_w8tSCOV4-pUwW_s3BFOK5IaJkcOEUf2mbp9VuDgh84LxX_IqqQZrA0MZUBY-fDt8gvYgcoiEaaouQfV2wHmKp5u7RHxxj6z9ruua666DfelhlnWbtJWrS0upGuVMrLyZXokX-prvosjxEcOxnF1ewbp-AA" alt="Apple annual revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>The post-pandemic/Apple silicon leap in 2021 is still notable, obviously. After three years of middling growth, 2025 kicked it into gear and then 2026 went wild—an increase of more than $62B in revenue, or 15.1%. It&#8217;s Apple&#8217;s best growth year since that magical 2021.</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/f1a21c23c9aedea8a69724eaa1874305.png?s=lY_JTsQwDIbfxVeapoxYpEqIR-DAEXEwwZ2GSeMQu0A16ruTEtYjlxy--N-OgCKkAj2Mqkl6a8W_OQ6cpXU82ddkHEelqFZHmkh-_m2VbsBUYtyIuRBo4BEVoT_C81wI5bD8Spi0_RuCKQUyg48YnccgdlO3T8KxOGGMM4Z_yaukGqwNDGVAWXj37fAB2j3zPhAmX12cyPWAkw_L1U2ieHKLUfqzrmsuuw7uSw_VLNukrZxBMTyUUrtud_E5t64vrMYbZS1vpheKM5mFl6-7wHx4QHcop6fnsK7v" alt="Apple annual revenue change" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>It&#8217;s also worth noting the boom-and-flat cycles Apple tends to go through, which are frequently driven by changes to iPhone buying. There was a big kick in 2015, another in 2018, a huge one in 2021 (amplified, again, by pandemic buying and Apple silicon), and a bit of one in 2026.</p>

<p>Of course, the driver of half of Apple&#8217;s revenue is the iPhone. As the iPhone goes, so goes Apple:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/2b1997edd0071489550d8e4e64f42d1a.png?s=lY_JTsQwDIbfxVeapoxYpEqIR-AwxxEHE9xpmNQOcQqMRn13UsJ65PrZ_3YCVKWs0MOYc9TeWvVvToIkbZ1M9jUaJ5yJs80jTaQ_d1ulKzCVGDdiKgQaeMSM0J_geS6EUjj-Sphy-zcEYwxkBs_IzmNQu6rbJxUuTsg8Y_iXvEqqwdLAUAaUhbtvhw_Q7kX2gTD66uJUbwecfDje3EXisy2y9hdd11x3HdyXHjknXSet5QyqkaGU2nSbq8-5dX1hNd74OAqTSfRCPNPXUxA5PKA7lL_zS1iWdw" alt="Annual iPhone revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>It&#8217;s safe to say that the iPhone 17 was a hit.</p>
<p>From a macro view, you can see the iPhone buying cycle here: booms in 2015 and 2021, then a plateauing until the next kick upward. This year was a big kick upward in iPhone revenue, even if you view the revenue figures in today&#8217;s dollars (which is now an option on our new, dynamic annual charts).</p>
<p>Now let&#8217;s look at the Mac, which followed up its record-breaking 2022 with a couple of years of regression, but has now had two straight years of solid growth:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/94ba8b91cd7864f817d3ce9201e38841.png?s=lY9LTsMwEIbvMlvi2FSISpEQR2DBsupiMJPG1PYYjwNUVe6Og3ku2X4z_-sMKEJFYICplCSD1uLeLHvO0lsO-jUpy7FQLLpMFEh-7rpJV6AaUXbCXAl08IgFYTjD81wJZX_6lRBK_zcEU_KkRhcxWode9Krun4RjdcIYZ_T_kjdJM1g6GOuAunD37fAB-gPzwRMm11ysyO2IwfnTzV2ieHGPUYYrY7qtMbCvPUrJsk5ayykUxWMttTGb68-5bX1lLV4FtCrTC8WZvj488_EB7bE-XW5hWd4B" alt="Annual Mac revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>During <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/hello-everyone-its-john-apples-q4-2026-earnings-call-transcript/">Monday&#8217;s earnings call</a>, new CEO John Ternus <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/he-admit-it-notes-from-another-record-apple-quarter/">pointed out</a> that Apple sold more Macs in fiscal 2026 than in any other year. That does provide a little bit of insight into the actual sales dynamics going on here, since Apple made more Mac <em>revenue</em> in fiscal 2022.</p>
<p>That means the average revenue per Mac sold in 2026 was lower, even as total revenue was up. The MacBook Neo is probably a big part of that dynamic, and lower-cost Mac minis getting sold to loads of people tasking them with agentic AI workflows also probably helped.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s been a rough comedown from pandemic highs for the iPad:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/5a62eba69d34b3d05cdd4a0bac996ffb.png?s=lY_LTsQwDEX_xVuaJhqNQKqE-AQWLBELK-NOg1InE7sMo1H_nZQgHku2x7r3-lwBRUgFBphUswzWSnj3KaYivU-zPWfjEyuxWp1oJvm52xbdgGnE-AlLJdDBARVhuMJpqYRKvPxamLX_O4I5RzJjYGQfMIrd0v2rJK5NyLxg_Fe8RVrB2sFYBarh83fDJ-iPKR0jYQ6txYs8jDiHeLl_zMQ3T8gy7J3r7pyDl_qHapFNaXvOoJg01qd2bnf7pdvsK2vzJmQ8mEJvxAvBun4A" alt="Annual iPad revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>After three consecutive years of lower sales, the iPad made a nice little rebound in 2025 and then was essentially flat in 2026. This is a product line that, while not showing explosive growth, has settled down into a solid $28B-a-year business. I do wonder if the upward revenue growth of this product is hindered by the fact that the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2025/10/m5-ipad-pro-review-promise-fulfilled/">iPad Pro is extremely expensive</a> and even very engaged iPad users can settle for <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2022/03/m1-ipad-air-review-a-familiar-face/">the cheaper iPad Air</a> without giving up much, if anything, in terms of functionality.</p>
<p>Now let&#8217;s look at Apple&#8217;s best performer over the last few years, Services:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/2a25d45730f3dc84edc84d350bfd1324.png?s=lY_LTsQwDEX_xVuaJhqNQKqE-AQWLBELK7jToNTJxO4wo1H_nZQgHku2x7r3-lwBRUgFBphUswzWSjj7FFOR3qfZvmfjEyuxWp1oJvm52xbdgGnE-AlLJdDBKyrCcIXjUgmVePm1MGv_dwRzjmTGwMg-YBS7pfs3SVybkHnB-K94i7SCtYOxClTD5--GT9AfUjpEwhxaixd5GHEO8XL_mIlvnpBl2DvX3TkHL_UP1SKb0vacQTFprE_t3O72S7fZV9bmjVA5BU9iCp2IF4J1_QA" alt="Annual Services revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>Apple&#8217;s growth rocket continues its hyperbolic trajectory. What&#8217;s next, $150B in a year? I wouldn&#8217;t bet against it.</p>
<p>Once a darling, Apple&#8217;s Wearables, Home and Accessories category has fallen on hard times:</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/96b940e7a1bc5dde3e78cdd896a83d02.png?s=lY_LTsQwDEX_xVuaJhqNQKqE-AQWLBELE9xpUOqE2J1hNOq_kxLEY8n2WPdenwugCKnAAJNqlsFaCe8-xVSk92m2p2x8YiVWqxPNJD9326IbMI0YP2GpBDp4QUUYLvC2VEIlnn8tzNr_HcGcI5kxMLIPGMVu6f5VEtcmZF4w_iveIq1g7WCsAtXw8bvhE_SHlA6RMIfW4kXuRpxDPN_eZ-KrB2QZ9s51N87BU_1DtcimtD1nUEwa61M7t7v-0m32lbV5cyIs-BxJTKEj8UKwrh8" alt="Annual Wearables, Home and Accessories revenue" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>Those eight straight years of annual growth were great, weren&#8217;t they? But since then, it&#8217;s three straight years of decline followed by an essentially flat 2026. Rumors have been swirling for years that Apple was gearing up for an onslaught of home products, and that <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/apple-announces-homeview-smart-display/">finally started to happen</a> <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/new-home-collection-banner-offers-apple-influenced-accessories/">this fall</a>. Perhaps this category will show some signs of life in 2027.</p>
<p>This brings us to the final chart, which I run here every year to properly contextualize all of Apple&#8217;s product lines. When I run the numbers as individual charts, they all seem more or less the same. They&#8217;re not. The iPhone dominates, and Services follows.</p>
